    story:"Kojo Mensah is a fourth-year medical student on his first surgical attachment.\n\nHis first morning ward round. Seven patients. The consultant moves fast.\n\nAt bed four — a 71-year-old man with a perforated duodenal ulcer. The consultant turns to Kojo.\n\n\"Should we operate?\"\n\nKojo knows the answer is yes — the man has a perforated viscus. Peritonitis. He will die without surgery.\n\n\"Yes,\" Kojo says.\n\n\"Correct. Now tell me what we need to know before we take him to theatre.\"\n\nKojo hesitates. The consultant does not wait.\n\n\"Cardiac function. Renal function. Respiratory reserve. Current medications — is he on warfarin? Clopidogrel? Does he have a pacemaker? What is his functional status — can he climb a flight of stairs? What does he want — has he got capacity, and does he understand that his risk of dying on the table is real?\"\n\nHe pauses at the bedside.\n\n\"Surgery is the easy part. I can open and close that abdomen in forty minutes. But if his heart stops on the table because we did not see his troponin was already elevated, or he bleeds out because no one stopped his anticoagulant, the operation is irrelevant.\"\n\nHe walks on.\n\n\"Perioperative medicine is what separates surgeons from technicians. Learn it.\"",
