        h:"✍️ Informed Consent & Surgical Ethics",
        a:"Consent is not a signature on a form. It is a process — a conversation between surgeon and patient that ensures the patient understands their diagnosis, the proposed operation, the alternatives, and the realistic risks. A patient who has signed a form but does not understand what was signed has not given valid consent. This distinction is both ethical and legal.",
        c:"VALID CONSENT REQUIRES:\n1. CAPACITY: patient must be able to understand, retain, weigh, and communicate the decision\nMental Capacity Act 2005 (UK) / Ghana equivalent: assume capacity unless proven otherwise\nCapacity is decision-specific and time-specific — a confused patient may lack capacity for one decision but not another\n\n2. INFORMATION: surgeon must disclose:\nDiagnosis and nature of proposed procedure\nExpected benefits\nMaterial risks — risks that a reasonable patient would want to know, even if rare\nAlternatives including no treatment\nConsequences of not having the operation\nMontgomery v Lanarkshire (2015 UK): clinician must disclose risks a reasonable patient in that patient's position would consider significant — departure from Bolam (what a reasonable doctor would disclose)\n\n3. VOLUNTARY: no coercion, no undue influence\n\nSPECIAL SITUATIONS:\nEmergency: if patient lacks capacity and life is threatened — treat in patient's best interests\nAdvance directive (living will): patient's previously expressed wishes when they had capacity — legally binding if valid and applicable\nLasting Power of Attorney (LPA): designated person makes health decisions — can consent or refuse on patient's behalf\nChildren under 16: Gillick competence — can consent if they understand the decision fully. Parental consent otherwise. If parents refuse life-saving treatment for child — court order\n\nDOCUMENTING CONSENT:\nConsent form: procedure, surgeon, risks discussed — specific risks named, not generic\nDate and time\nInterpreter used if language barrier\nNotes in patient record of the conversation — consent form alone is insufficient documentation\n\nWITHDRAWAL OF CONSENT:\nPatient can withdraw consent at any time, including in theatre — must stop procedure if feasible and safe\n\nRISK DISCLOSURE FRAMEWORK:\nCommon risks (>1 in 10): always disclose\nSerious risks (even if rare): always disclose — paralysis, death, loss of organ\nProcedure-specific risks: e.g. recurrent laryngeal nerve injury for thyroidectomy, anastomotic leak for bowel surgery, bile duct injury for cholecystectomy",
