        h:"💧 Surgical Fluid Management & Electrolytes",
        a:"Fluid management in surgical patients is one of the most common and most poorly performed clinical tasks in medicine. Both under-resuscitation and over-resuscitation cause harm. The goal is not to give as much fluid as possible — it is to give the right fluid, in the right amount, at the right time.",
        c:"FLUID COMPARTMENTS:\nTotal body water (TBW): 60% of body weight in adult male (55% female)\nIntracellular fluid (ICF): 2/3 of TBW\nExtracellular fluid (ECF): 1/3 of TBW → Interstitial (3/4 ECF) + Intravascular (1/4 ECF = plasma)\n\nFLUID TYPES:\n\nCRYSTALLOIDS:\nNormal saline (0.9% NaCl): Na 154, Cl 154. Distributes throughout ECF (25% stays intravascular). Risk: hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis with large volumes\nHartmann's/Ringer's lactate: balanced crystalloid — Na 131, Cl 111, K 5, Ca 2, lactate 29. More physiological. Preferred for resuscitation\nDextrose 5%: distributes throughout TBW (only 8% stays intravascular). For free water replacement and drug delivery — NOT for resuscitation\n\nCOLLOIDS:\nHuman albumin solution (HAS) 4-5%: stays intravascular longer. Used in SBP, hepatic disease, large-volume paracentesis\nGelatin (Gelofusine): synthetic colloid — stays intravascular 2-4h. Use with caution — anaphylaxis risk\nStarches (HES): withdrawn from most markets — increased AKI and mortality in sepsis (CHEST, 6S trials)\n\nSURGICAL FLUID ASSESSMENT:\nHOLLIDAY-SEGAR MAINTENANCE (children): 4ml/kg/h first 10kg + 2ml/kg/h next 10kg + 1ml/kg/h remainder\nAdult maintenance: approximately 25-30ml/kg/day\nSurgical losses: 3rd spacing (major abdominal surgery 5-10ml/kg/h) + estimated blood loss + insensible losses (fever adds 300ml/°C/day)\n\nGOAL-DIRECTED FLUID THERAPY:\nFluid challenge: 250-500ml bolus → reassess cardiac output, stroke volume variation, pulse pressure variation\nResponders (stroke volume increases >10%): preload-dependent — give more fluid\nNon-responders: fluid overloaded or cardiac failure — stop fluids, consider vasopressor\n\nELECTROLYTE DISORDERS (surgical):\nHYPONATRAEMIA (<135): dilutional (SIADH, fluid overload), hypovolaemic (NG losses, bowel prep). Correct slowly — rapid correction → central pontine myelinolysis\nHYPERNATRAEMIA (>145): inadequate free water (nil by mouth, fever, insensible losses). Correct with hypotonic fluids, slowly\nHYPOKALAEMIA (<3.5): vomiting, NG drainage, diarrhoea, diuretics. Replace K+ (max 20mmol/h IV, cardiac monitoring)\nHYPERKALAEMIA (>5.5): AKI, tissue destruction, ACE inhibitors. ECG — peaked T waves, QRS widening. Treat: calcium gluconate (membrane stabilisation), insulin-dextrose, salbutamol, calcium resonium",
